- id: role-write-boundary
  given: >-
    An admitted Build with its Candidate worktree, harness home and a spaceless per-Build temp
    directory, on a route whose roles use both Claude Code and Codex. Outside the grants there are
    the control checkout (tracked files, `.kogen/`, Git metadata), a second retained Candidate and
    its harness home, the owner records, both login scopes, a symlink and a `/tmp` alias path that
    point outside, and a test-owned sentinel directory. The fake role executable
    `test/support/fake_boundary_role` tries the writes below and records each result in a receipt
    under the harness home.
  when: >-
    The controller launches the fresh Developer, a helper child and a `make` grandchild, an exact
    resume, the Reviewer and a cross-harness Expert through `mix kogen.expert`, each through the
    real adapter launch path.
  then: >-
    Every role process runs as `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec -p <profile> ...`. The profile is one
    Build-wide profile, `Kogen.Build.WriteBoundary.render/1`, built at admission from canonical
    paths. It has `(allow default)` and `(deny file-write*)`, and writes are allowed only under these
    paths: the Candidate; the harness home; the Build temp dir, which is also every launch's
    `TMPDIR`, `TMPPREFIX` and `CLAUDE_CODE_TMPDIR`; the stdio devices; and the named
    shared-login-state exceptions (the login keychain file and its `.sb-` temp files,
    `<Claude scope>/.oauth_refresh.lock`, and the Codex scope as scenario codex-roles-inside-boundary
    states). The controller's `KOGEN_RAW_LOG_DIR` is not granted; role launches get
    `KOGEN_RAW_LOG_DIR=<harness home>/raw-log`, which the controller copies into its own at every
    Build exit. `/bin/ps` is the only
    no-sandbox exec. `lsopen` and `appleevent-send` are denied. From inside each role, a helper,
    the `make` grandchild and a background child that outlives the role, writes into the Candidate,
    the harness home and the temp dir succeed. Each of these writes fails with EPERM and leaves
    the bytes unchanged: create, append, delete, rename, chmod, hardlink or symlink-follow into
    the control checkout (including `.kogen/` and `.kogen/build.lock`); `git commit`,
    `git update-ref` and `git config` against control's Git metadata; the controller's own
    `KOGEN_RAW_LOG_DIR` (placed inside control's `.kogen/runtime/` in the test); the other Candidate and its
    harness home; an owner record; the Claude scope's `settings.json`; the sentinel; the same
    paths through their `/tmp` aliases. A nested `sandbox-exec` with a permissive profile exits
    71. The tracking record's `boundary` block has mode `applied`, the profile's sha256 and the
    grant list. In the real fixture Build, a project SessionStart hook (in the fixture only) inside
    the real Claude Code Developer (fresh and resumed) and Reviewer process trees records
    successful writes inside the grants: the Build temp dir for every session, and the Candidate's
    ignored `.kogen/runtime/` for Developer sessions only, so the Reviewer never changes the
    Candidate. The same hook's write into an existing directory of the fixture's control checkout
    (`.kogen/runtime/boundary-escape/`) and its `git update-ref` into control's refs are refused
    with "Operation not permitted". Neither the file nor the ref exists afterwards, and the nested
    record's boundary mode is `applied`.
  wrong_result: >-
    The boundary wraps only the first Developer launch, not resumes, the Reviewer or the Expert; it
    uses non-canonical paths, so the grants silently never match; it grants `/private/tmp`, `$HOME`
    or control; it relies on Claude Code permissions or Codex's own sandbox, which don't cover the
    harness process's own writes; or Kogen asserts on the launch arguments while the real process
    is unconfined.
  verified_by: [check, live-shape-to-build]
  evidence: >-
    write_boundary_test.exs renders the profile for Claude-only, Codex-only and hybrid routes and
    asserts the exact grant list. It then runs real `sandbox-exec` launches through the Claude and
    Codex adapters with `KOGEN_HARNESS` naming `fake_boundary_role`, which runs every write, a
    `make` grandchild, a `nohup` child, a nested `sandbox-exec`, an `open -g -a` of a test app
    (LaunchServices) and an `osascript` Apple Event, and writes a JSON receipt of exit codes and
    errno values to the harness home. The receipt must show `open` and `osascript` failing. The test asserts each result, the unchanged bytes (sha256) of
    every protected path, the absence of every attempted outside file and ref, and a control launch
    where the same inside writes succeed. harness_role_test.exs covers the Expert launch from the
    Developer's tree. The paid run adds the fixture-only `.claude/settings.json` SessionStart probe
    through `KOGEN_RAW_LOG_DIR`, and live_shape_to_build_test.exs asserts the receipts per session
    id, the absent escape file and ref, and the record's boundary block.
  proof:
    offline: [test/kogen/write_boundary_test.exs, test/kogen/harness_role_test.exs, test/kogen/claude_code_harness_test.exs, rehearse-live-shape-to-build]
    paid_target: live-shape-to-build
    paid_reason: >-
      provider-required: live-shape-to-build; observation: the real managed Claude Code 2.1.281
      Developer (fresh and exact resume) and Reviewer run to acceptance inside the boundary, and a
      write from their own process trees into the fixture's control checkout is refused while a
      write into the Candidate succeeds; offline-limit: a fake harness cannot show that the real
      runtime's own writes (sessions, OAuth refresh, shell snapshots, task output) stay inside the
      grants, or that the real launch path wraps the real process tree
    affected_paths: [lib/kogen/build/write_boundary.ex, lib/kogen/build.ex, lib/kogen/build/tracking.ex, lib/kogen/harness.ex, lib/kogen/harness/claude.ex, lib/kogen/harness/codex.ex, lib/kogen/claude_code.ex, lib/kogen/codex/environment.ex, lib/mix/tasks/kogen.expert.ex, test/kogen/write_boundary_test.exs, test/kogen/harness_role_test.exs, test/kogen/claude_code_harness_test.exs, test/kogen/live_shape_to_build_test.exs, test/support/fake_boundary_role, test/support/fake_claude, test/support/fake_codex, README.md, workflows/claude-code-runtime-upgrade.md, priv/kogen/test-reliability.yaml, priv/kogen/test-reliability-remediation.yaml]

- id: write-boundary-fails-closed
  given: >-
    Build admissions where `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec` is replaced by a missing path; where
    `KOGEN_WORKSPACES_ROOT` is given through the `/tmp` alias of a `/private/tmp` directory; where
    a grant can't be resolved to an existing directory; where the
    rendered grants would include `/`, `/private/tmp`, `/private/var`, `$HOME`, the control root or
    an ancestor of it; and where the
    admission self-test of the profile fails. There are also a controller that already runs inside
    a Kogen boundary (with `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY` set by it), one inside a foreign sandbox (no
    marker), and an unconfined controller with a spoofed `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY`.
  when: Each Build is admitted and reaches its first role launch.
  then: >-
    Every grant is rendered from its resolved canonical path, so the aliased root admits normally,
    its profile names only `/private/tmp/...` forms, and a fake role's write inside its Candidate
    succeeds. The unresolvable grant, the forbidden grants, the
    missing `sandbox-exec` and the failed self-test stop before any readiness call or role launch,
    with a message naming the reason and the path. Nothing is launched unconfined, and the Candidate is kept as in
    failure-retention. Confinement is detected by the kernel self-test: applying
    `(version 1)(allow default)` to `/usr/bin/true` exits 71 only inside a sandbox. An env var alone
    never decides confinement. When the self-test reports confinement and the marker is set, a
    `mix kogen.expert` launch and every role of a fixture Build whose roles are `KOGEN_HARNESS`
    test executables run without re-applying a profile, the record's boundary mode is `inherited`,
    and a fake role of that nested Build still can't write outside the enclosing profile's grants.
    A confined `mix kogen.build` whose roles use a managed runtime stops before admission with
    "a Build cannot start inside another Build's role boundary". With confinement and no marker,
    the Build stops naming the foreign sandbox. With no confinement,
    the marker is ignored and the profile is applied (`applied`). Every role launch sets
    `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY` to the profile sha256. The controller's own verification children
    (`make check`, the selected targets and any proof-selector or base-workspace run, all started by
    `Kogen.Build.VerificationRunner`) run outside the boundary with `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY` removed
    from their environment (it joins `VerificationRunner.scrubbed_names/0`), so fixture Builds inside
    them apply their own boundary.
    At every Build exit, including stops, the controller copies `<harness home>/raw-log/` into its
    own `KOGEN_RAW_LOG_DIR` when one is set.
  wrong_result: >-
    A missing or failing `sandbox-exec` falls back to an unwrapped launch; a `/tmp` alias is
    accepted and never matches; the marker alone switches the boundary off; a real nested Build
    runs `inherited` with another Build's grants; or the controller's
    `make check` runs inside a role boundary, so every fixture Build's `sandbox-exec` fails with
    `sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted` (the 2026-09-19 failure of 47 tests).
  verified_by: [check]
  evidence: >-
    write_boundary_test.exs covers each refusal with a launch sentinel that must stay absent,
    plus a Build whose workspaces root is given through the `/tmp` alias: its rendered profile
    must contain only canonical paths and its fake role's inside write must succeed (a grant the
    kernel never matches would make it fail),
    starts one fake-harness fixture Build from inside a real outer `sandbox-exec` (with and without
    the marker, asserting `inherited` and a refused write outside the enclosing grants), and a
    managed-runtime Build (a fake runtime executable under a disposable `KOGEN_CLAUDE_ROOT`) from
    inside it, asserting the refusal. It also spoofs the marker in an unconfined run and asserts
    the raw-log copy on success and on a stop. candidate_verification_test.exs asserts that the
    environment of the controller's verification child lacks `KOGEN_WRITE_BOUNDARY` and that a
    fixture Build started by that child records `applied`.
  proof:
    offline: [test/kogen/write_boundary_test.exs, test/kogen/candidate_verification_test.exs]
    paid_target: none
    paid_reason: >-
      offline-sufficient: activation failures, grant validation, kernel confinement detection and
      the controller's unconfined verification children are host facts exercised with the real
      sandbox-exec; no provider semantics
    affected_paths: [lib/kogen/build/write_boundary.ex, lib/kogen/build.ex, lib/kogen/build/**, lib/kogen/harness.ex, test/kogen/write_boundary_test.exs, test/kogen/candidate_verification_test.exs, test/test_helper.exs, priv/kogen/test-reliability.yaml, priv/kogen/test-reliability-remediation.yaml]

