    94	- id: approved-copy-in-candidate
    95	  scenario_ids: [candidate-creation, publication-unmoved-main, publication-refused, shaping-during-build]
    96	  description: >-
    97	    Roles read the Approved package in their cwd, but it is ignored and not in the worktree
    98	    checkout. A missing copy breaks roles, and an editable copy used as authority would let a
    99	    Developer rewrite the contract.
   100	  ownership:
   101	    - paths: "<Candidate>/.kogen/intents/approved/<slug>/"
   102	      when_exists: "Written at admission from the controller's frozen in-memory Approved bytes; a pre-existing path means the Candidate is not fresh and admission refuses."
   103	      owner_after_creation: "The controller."
   104	      owner_during_operation: "Read-only for roles; the frozen bytes remain authority, and publication packages from them."
   105	      permitted_mutation: "None by roles. The controller removes it with the Candidate."
   106	      validation: "The existing approved-mutation check applies to this copy; a mismatch is an integrity failure."
   107	      git_state: "Ignored in the Candidate; control's own Approved copy is unchanged until successful publication removes it."
   108	      upgrade_behavior: "None."
   109	
   110	- id: shared-cwd-assumptions
   111	  scenario_ids: [candidate-routing, publication-unmoved-main]
   112	  description: >-
   113	    About fifteen offline test files run Build.run inside File.cd!(repo) and assert on repo's
   114	    HEAD. They keep passing if Build silently works in the invoking checkout, so they must also
   115	    assert that a Candidate existed and was the role cwd. Their test-reliability source_sha256
   116	    bindings must be refreshed with scripts/check/refresh_test_reliability_sources.py.
   117	
   118	- id: boundary-breaks-existing-fixtures
   119	  scenario_ids: [role-write-boundary, write-boundary-fails-closed, shaping-during-build]
   120	  description: >-
   121	    Every fake-harness Build in check now runs its fake roles inside a real Seatbelt profile. Any
   122	    existing fixture whose fake role, fake hook or helper script writes outside the Candidate, the
   123	    harness home or the Build temp dir gets EPERM (known at 9ff7af6e: the midbuild-config-mutation.sh
   124	    hook of two_outer_resumptions_test.exs and the control-side writes of shaping-during-build). In
   125	    the 2026-09-19 containment attempt, 47 tests failed when fixture Builds ran inside an outer
   126	    profile. Mitigation: the controller's make check is unconfined, so fixture Builds apply their own
   127	    profile; moved writes go to the trusted test process; no grant is widened and no fake executable
   128	    is special-cased; the Developer first inventories every fake role and hook with its write paths
   129	    (handoff notes, checked by Review). Inside the Developer's own shell, fixture Builds run inherited;
   130	    probe 9 showed today's whole suite gives the same result inside and outside the profile (800/805,
   131	    the same five pre-existing environment failures, so not a pass).
   132	
   133	- id: seatbelt-interface-deprecated
   134	  scenario_ids: [role-write-boundary, write-boundary-fails-closed]
   135	  description: >-
   136	    `sandbox-exec` and SBPL are undocumented and marked deprecated, although Apple still ships them
   137	    and uses the same kernel profiles. A macOS release could remove or change them. Failure is
   138	    closed: a missing binary or a failed admission self-test stops the Build before any launch,
   139	    naming the reason, and there is no unwrapped fallback. Both runtime-upgrade workflows re-run the
   140	    boundary probes, and a macOS upgrade on the Build host should too (README).
   141	
   142	- id: harness-write-drift
   143	  scenario_ids: [role-write-boundary, codex-roles-inside-boundary]
   144	  description: >-
   145	    The grants cover what Claude Code 2.1.281 and Codex 0.156.1 wrote in the probes. A new runtime
   146	    could write elsewhere, for example a new cache dir under the real HOME, and the role would then
   147	    fail on EPERM. Kogen gives no broader grant. Readiness runs inside the boundary, both
   148	    runtime-upgrade workflows re-probe, and the denial is logged by the kernel
   149	    (`log show --predicate 'eventMessage CONTAINS "deny(1) file-write"'`, named in README).
   150	    Denials of a role's own shell-profile writes (zsh completion caches, mise state under HOME) are
   151	    harmless and expected noise.
   152	
   153	- id: role-writable-login-state
   154	  scenario_ids: [role-write-boundary, codex-roles-inside-boundary]
   155	  description: >-
   156	    For logins to keep working, roles can write the login keychain file (Claude Code's OAuth
   157	    refresh runs /usr/bin/security in-process; probe 12), the Claude refresh lock, and most of the
   158	    Codex scope (auth.json refresh, rollouts, config.toml bookkeeping). A role could therefore add a
   159	    keychain item or corrupt its own login, but not take over another tool: Keychain item ACLs
   160	    still apply; the Codex discovery and Kogen-owned entries are denied; config.toml is validated
   161	    by native_settings.py at every Kogen open. Reads, including of credentials, are not confined by
   162	    this Intent (SEC-01 read isolation and SEC-02 are later).
   163	
   164	- id: controller-verification-unconfined
   165	  scenario_ids: [write-boundary-fails-closed, controller-judges-candidate]
   166	  description: >-
   167	    The controller runs `make check` and the selected targets on Candidate code outside the
   168	    boundary. A Developer can't confine that code, but it can write it, so a malicious test would
   169	    run with the user's full write access during verification. This is deliberate: fixture Builds
   170	    inside check and the live targets must apply their own profile, which the kernel refuses inside
   171	    another profile, and live targets must update logins and retained evidence. Review and the
   172	    guarded-path check see such code before publication; confining verification needs a
   173	    nesting-free design (for example per-target profiles with fixture Builds disabled) and belongs
   174	    with process custody (ROADMAP order 7).
   175	
   176	- id: inherited-mode
   177	  scenario_ids: [write-boundary-fails-closed]
   178	  description: >-
   179	    Inside a role profile the kernel refuses a second profile, so `mix kogen.expert` from a role and
   180	    offline fixture Builds whose roles are KOGEN_HARNESS test executables run `inherited`: they are
   181	    bounded by the enclosing Build's grants, not their own. A managed-runtime Build refuses to start
   182	    confined, so no real Build ever runs with another Build's grants. Only the kernel self-test plus
   183	    the marker enables inherited; a foreign sandbox without the marker stops, and a marker without
   184	    confinement is ignored.
   185	
   186	- id: service-delegation
   187	  scenario_ids: [role-write-boundary]
   188	  description: >-
   189	    The profile allows everything except file writes. Tested delegation routes are closed (launchctl
   190	    bootstrap and submit refused; LaunchServices and Apple Events denied by rule), but other XPC or
   191	    Mach services a role can reach might write on its behalf; no such route was found and none is
   192	    claimed closed. A hardlink already present in a granted tree would let a role write its target;
   193	    admission creates every granted tree fresh without hardlinks (deps copy never `cp -l`). A
   194	    deny-default profile would narrow this but failed Builds on 2026-09-18/19; it is a candidate for
   195	    SEC-02.
   196	
   197	- id: unrun-live-targets
   198	  scenario_ids: [same-candidate-rework, per-build-harness-home]
   199	  description: >-
   200	    live-general runs native launches (no Build) that now go through the changed Claude
   201	    environment; it is not run here, and its owner file is not edited. live-reviewer-rework is now
   202	    selected (scenario codex-roles-inside-boundary), so its hybrid nested Build, with a worktree, a
   203	    per-Build home, the write boundary and a Codex Reviewer whose operation root is in that home,
   204	    has a real run in this Intent. The next Build that selects live-general is its first real run
   205	    on this code.
   206	
   207	- id: space-in-workspace-path
   208	  scenario_ids: [candidate-creation, same-candidate-rework, per-build-harness-home]
   209	  description: >-
   210	    The default root contains a space ("Application Support"). Any unquoted path in a Make recipe,
   211	    sh -c string, hook command or test helper breaks only in real use. test_helper.exs uses a
   212	    spaced root for every run, including the live fixture. Known instance:
   213	    test/kogen/lifecycle_test.exs:724-792 (install_target_evidence_fixture!/1, -pa join at line 785, at 9ff7af6e) splices unquoted -pa paths.

